What you can now explain to an interviewer:

What a call option is and why it has a price
What Black-Scholes is actually computing (discounted expectation under Q)
Why the drift mu disappears (hedging argument, no-arbitrage)
What Monte Carlo is doing and why it converges at 1/sqrt(N)
Why you used OOP design (encapsulation, extensibility)

Here's a comprehensive interview guide for this project. I've organised it by the questions you're actually likely to get, from "explain your project" down to the deep follow-ups that separate people who memorised from people who understand.

1. The 60-second project summary
Practice saying this fluently — it's your opening:

"I built a European option pricer in C++ that prices a call two independent ways: the Black-Scholes closed-form formula, and Monte Carlo simulation. The point was to validate one against the other — Monte Carlo should converge to the analytical price as the number of simulations grows, at a rate of 1 over root N, and I verified that empirically. It's object-oriented: an Option class holds the contract parameters and exposes both pricing methods. The C++ writes results to a CSV, and a Python notebook reads that and plots the convergence on a log-log scale to confirm the error decays at the theoretical rate."

That hits: what, why, the design, and the result. Everything below is the follow-up they'll dig into.

2. The finance — what they'll probe
What is a call option?
The right, but not the obligation, to buy an asset at a fixed strike price K at expiry T. You pay a premium upfront. Payoff at expiry is max(S_T − K, 0).

Why does it have a price / why is the payoff asymmetric?
Downside is capped (you can walk away, losing only the premium), upside is unbounded. That asymmetry has value, so it costs something today.

Call vs put. Call = right to buy, profits when price rises, payoff max(S_T − K, 0). Put = right to sell, profits when price falls, payoff max(K − S_T, 0).

Payoff vs profit. Payoff is what you get at exercise. Profit subtracts the premium. You exercise whenever S_T > K even if you haven't recovered the premium — partial recovery beats zero. Break-even is K + premium.

European vs American. European can only be exercised at expiry. American can be exercised any time up to expiry. Yours is European — that's what makes the closed form exist. (Good to know: American options generally need numerical methods like binomial trees or least-squares Monte Carlo.)

3. The model — Geometric Brownian Motion
Why model the stock as random?
Prices aren't predictable. GBM is the standard model:

dS_t = μ S_t dt + σ S_t dW_t

μ = drift (average return)
σ = volatility
W_t = Brownian motion, increments W_t − W_s ~ N(0, t−s), independent
Why is the noise scaled by S_t?
Because what's stable across stocks is percentage returns, not absolute moves. A £10 stock and a £1000 stock both move ~2% a day, not ±£0.20 each. Dividing through gives dS/S = μ dt + σ dW — the return is what's random.

Solution to the SDE:

S_T = S_0 exp[(μ − ½σ²)T + σ√T · Z], Z ~ N(0,1)

So S_T is log-normally distributed.

Where does the −½σ² come from? Itô's lemma. When you apply the stochastic chain rule to log(S_t), you must keep the second-order term because (dW)² = dt doesn't vanish. That term contributes −½σ². Intuitively it's the drag from volatility: up 10% then down 10% leaves you at 0.99, not 1. (If they push: it's Jensen's inequality on the convex exponential.)

4. The deep one — risk-neutral pricing
This is the question that matters most. Expect: "Why does the drift μ disappear from Black-Scholes?"

The answer:
You can form a portfolio of the option plus a continuously-adjusted quantity Δ of the underlying so that the random dW term cancels exactly. That portfolio is instantaneously riskless, so by no-arbitrage it must earn the risk-free rate r. When you write that condition down, μ drops out entirely and r takes its place.

What is arbitrage? A guaranteed profit with zero risk and zero net investment. In efficient markets it gets competed away instantly, so "no-arbitrage" is the assumption that pins down a unique fair price.

The risk-neutral measure Q.
Equivalently: there's a probability measure Q under which every asset drifts at r instead of μ. The price of any derivative is:

V_0 = e^(−rT) · E^Q[payoff]

Discount the expected payoff — but take the expectation under Q, not the real world.

Why not just use the real-world expectation? Because different investors have different beliefs about μ, which would give different prices. Q is the unique measure forced by no-arbitrage, so it makes the price objective. Q is not a forecast — it's a pricing device.

The intuition for "risk-neutral": under Q, investors are indifferent to risk, so every asset earns r. It's not that the world is actually risk-neutral; it's that derivative prices come out the same as if it were.

5. Black-Scholes formula itself
C = S·N(d₁) − K·e^(−rT)·N(d₂)

d₁ = [ln(S/K) + (r + ½σ²)T] / (σ√T), d₂ = d₁ − σ√T

N(d₂) = the risk-neutral probability the option finishes in the money (S_T > K).
K·e^(−rT)·N(d₂) = present value of paying the strike, weighted by the probability you actually pay it.
S·N(d₁) = present value of receiving the stock, conditional and size-weighted.
N(·) = standard normal CDF.
The five inputs: S, K, r, σ, T. Note what's not there: μ. And of these five, σ is the only one you can't directly observe — which leads to implied volatility (below).

6. Monte Carlo
The algorithm:

Draw Z ~ N(0,1)
S_T = S·exp[(r − ½σ²)T + σ√T·Z] — note r, not μ, because we simulate under Q
payoff = max(S_T − K, 0)
Repeat N times, average, multiply by e^(−rT)
Why does it work? Law of large numbers: the sample mean converges to E^Q[payoff], which is exactly what risk-neutral pricing says to compute.

Convergence rate: 1/√N. Standard error = σ_payoff / √N. To halve the error you need 4× the simulations. Slow, but dimension-independent — which is why MC wins for high-dimensional problems (baskets of many assets) where PDE/tree methods blow up.

Key trade-off — why bother with MC if BS is exact?
BS only has a closed form for simple payoffs. MC prices anything — path-dependent options (Asian, barrier), multi-asset baskets, exotic payoffs. For your project, MC's job is to validate BS; in practice it's the tool you reach for when no formula exists.

Why the fixed seed (42)? Reproducibility — same seed gives the same sequence every run, which makes debugging and grading deterministic. In production you'd want a varying seed (or run many independent batches) so results aren't always identical.

7. The C++ / software side
Be ready to justify every design choice:

Why object-oriented?
Encapsulation — the Option bundles its data (S, K, r, σ, T) with the methods that act on it, instead of passing five loose parameters everywhere. It's extensible: adding a put, or Greeks, is just another method. It models the domain naturally — an option is a thing that knows how to price itself.

Header vs source file. The .h declares the interface (what exists); the .cpp defines the implementation (the logic). This lets other files use the class by including only the header, and lets you recompile files independently.

Include guards (#ifndef / #define / #endif). Stop a header being processed twice in one translation unit, which would cause redefinition errors.

const methods. blackScholesPrice() const promises not to mutate the object. It lets the method be called on const objects and documents intent.

Constructor + initialiser list (: S(S), K(K), ...). Initialises members directly rather than assigning inside the body — the preferred, slightly more efficient idiom.

static helper (normalCDF). File-local — it's an implementation detail, not part of the public interface, so it's hidden from other files.

The normal CDF trick. C++ has no built-in N(x), but <cmath> has erfc. The exact identity N(x) = ½·erfc(−x/√2) gives it to you with no approximation.

std::mt19937 + std::normal_distribution. The engine (Mersenne Twister) produces raw random bits; the distribution shapes them into N(0,1) draws. Separating the two is the modern C++ random design.

Precomputing drift/diffusion outside the loop. They don't depend on the loop variable, so computing them once instead of N times is a simple optimisation.

The Makefile. Automates compilation. -std=c++17 (modern features like range-based for), -Wall (all warnings), -O2 (optimisation), -Iinclude (where to find headers). One make instead of a long g++ command, and it only rebuilds what changed.

8. Likely curveballs
Have an answer ready for these:

"What are the assumptions of Black-Scholes, and where do they break?"
Constant volatility and interest rate, log-normal prices, no transaction costs, continuous trading, no jumps. Reality breaks all of these — most importantly volatility isn't constant, which shows up as the volatility smile: implied vol varies with strike, whereas BS assumes it's flat.

"What is implied volatility?"
σ is the one input you can't observe. Given a market price, you invert Black-Scholes to find the σ that reproduces it. That's implied vol — the market's forward-looking estimate. Inverting requires a root-finder (e.g. Newton-Raphson) since you can't solve for σ algebraically.

"What are the Greeks?"
Sensitivities of the price to inputs. Delta = ∂C/∂S (and N(d₁) is the call's delta — the hedge ratio from the derivation). Gamma = ∂²C/∂S². Vega = ∂C/∂σ. Theta = ∂C/∂T. Rho = ∂C/∂r. These are how you actually risk-manage a book.

"How would you make the Monte Carlo more accurate without more simulations?"
Variance reduction. Antithetic variates: for each Z, also use −Z; the negative correlation cuts variance. Control variates: use a related quantity with known expectation to correct the estimate. Good answer because it shows you understand the 1/√N wall.

"How would you verify correctness?"
MC converges to BS within Monte Carlo standard error (and I checked the error decays as 1/√N on a log-log plot). Could also check put-call parity, or known boundary cases (deep in/out of the money).

"Put-call parity?"
C − P = S − K·e^(−rT). A model-free no-arbitrage relationship — lets you price a put from a call instantly, and is a sanity check on any pricer.

"What's the time complexity?" MC is O(N) — linear in simulations, which your timing plot confirms. BS is O(1).

"Why C++ and not Python for the pricing?" Speed. MC is millions of independent iterations — a tight compiled loop is orders of magnitude faster than interpreted Python. Industry quant libraries are C++ for exactly this. Python's role here is analysis/visualisation, where speed doesn't matter and the ecosystem is richer.

9. The honest weaknesses (say these before they find them)
Showing you know the limitations is more impressive than pretending there are none:

European calls only — no puts, no American, no exotics yet.
No input validation (negative σ, T = 0 would misbehave).
Single-threaded — MC is embarrassingly parallel and could be threaded.
Hardcoded parameters rather than command-line/file input.
Fixed seed — fine for a demo, not for production estimates.
Then pivot to roadmap: puts via put-call parity, the Greeks, antithetic variates, multithreading. That turns a weakness question into a "here's how I think about extending it" answer.

